# RECORD-GUARD-RULE: issue 89, and the four unguarded launchers it found on its first run

The rule of issue 89, by AST: any `.py` under `research/records/` that reaches the model carries
the refusal guard, or the suite fails.

## What it found

The issue said "the next miss is invisible". It had already happened, twice, in records committed
that same day. The rule's first run named four files with no guard:

| file | what running it does |
| --- | --- |
| `LIVE-LENS-INFRASTRUCTURE-2026-09-07/acceptance-v1.py` | takes the primary model-run lock and loads the checkpoint, at module scope |
| `LIVE-LENS-INFRASTRUCTURE-2026-09-07/benchmark-v1.py` | takes the primary model-run lock and loads the checkpoint, at module scope |
| `HISTORY-CACHE-2026-09-07/prepare_corpus.py` | imports `mlx_lm` inside `main`, initialising Metal |
| `HISTORY-CACHE-2026-09-07/prepare_reuse_corpus.py` | imports `mlx_lm` inside `main`, initialising Metal |

The first two are the serious ones. Neither has an `if __name__ == "__main__"` block at all: every
statement runs on import, the checkpoint path is absolute, `runlock.PROJECT_ROOT` is repointed at
the primary checkout, and `hold_model_run_lock` is called on line 13. `python benchmark-v1.py`
loads the 4B model on the Director's laptop and holds the primary lock. This patch adds the
standard guard to all four.

## Three decisions worth the reviewer's attention

**1. `mlx` counts, not only `mlx_lm`.** The issue names `mlx_lm`. `benchmark-v1.py` imports only
`mlx.core`, and reaches the checkpoint through `runlock.load_weights` rather than `load_policy`:
an `mlx_lm`-only rule misses the worst file in the tree. The hazard being guarded is Metal
initialisation and the lock, and `import mlx` is both. This is wider than the issue's letter and
is flagged rather than done quietly.

**2. Imports at any scope, unlike the closure walker beside it.** `_imports_that_execute` ignores
function-local imports on purpose, because it asks what runs at import time. This rule asks
whether *running the file* can reach the model, and `main()`'s body runs.
`prepare_corpus.py` hides its only `mlx_lm` import inside `main`; a module-scope-only rule clears
it.

**3. The rule is one-directional.** Reaching the model requires a guard; a guard does not require
reaching the model. `jlens-hosted-qwen35-4b-2026-09-05/convert_lens.py` is guarded and reaches
nothing, and stays passing.

## The execution check earns its place

Issue 89 asked for the guard's placement to be verified by executing the file rather than parsing
it. That turned out to be the load-bearing half. Three mutations were run against the finished
rule:

| mutation | caught by |
| --- | --- |
| the four new guards removed | the AST rule, naming all four |
| a guard moved below the import it guards | the AST rule, naming the line it now follows |
| a guard whose condition is inverted, so it never fires | **only** the execution check |

The third is the point. `if __name__ == "__main__" and "--i-am-a-record" in _sys.argv:` — one
word deleted — is a guard by every structural test and refuses nothing. The AST rule passed it.
The subprocess died on `mlx` and the test said so.

Which is why the execution is made safe rather than trusted. Each script runs with a `PYTHONPATH`
whose `mlx`, `mlx_lm` and `local_llm_lab` are stub packages that raise on import. If a guard does
not fire, the child dies on the stub instead of loading a checkpoint or taking the primary lock,
and the stub's marker in stderr is what turns the test red. Children are started through
`local_llm_lab.spawn` so they arrive by `posix_spawn` (R45).

`compile()` is called as well as `ast.parse`, because a guard placed above `from __future__`
parses cleanly and raises `SyntaxError` only under compilation — the way the first hand-written
guard passed review while being broken. That has its own fixture.

## The negative fixture

`TRAIN-COST-2026-09-05/build_page.py` writes an HTML page whose prose names both `mlx_lm` and
`evaluate.load_policy`. It imports neither and calls neither. The test asserts all three facts
together: the strings are present, the AST finds no site, and the rule passes it. A substring
scanner flags it, someone switches the rule off, and the rule protects nothing after that.

## Cost

`research/records` is not lint-clean and never has been: 67 ruff findings across these four files
before this patch. The guards add four, all `I001` (`import sys as _sys` above the rest), which is
the same finding the three hand-guarded records already carry. It is the structural price of a
guard that must precede the imports it guards.

## Verification

```
